- How would you handle deletions or moving points?
- What are worst-case scenarios if density spikes in a single bucket, and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of space-time trade-offs in streaming analysis. Introduces hashing-based approximation and how it affects accuracy, latency, and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Hash bucketing for compact counts
- Amortized batch updates
- Probability of collision vs. accuracy
- Space-time trade-offs vs. exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
class BucketCounter {
  constructor(D) {
    this.D = D;
    this.buckets = new Array(D).fill(0);
    this.max = 0;
  }
  updateBatch(batch, hashFn) {
    for (const c of batch) {
      const b = hashFn(c) % this.D;
      this.buckets[b] += 1;
      if (this.buckets[b] > this.max) this.max = this.buckets[b];
    }
  }
  query(t) { return this.max >= t; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you bound false positives and choose D for a target ε? 
- What changes if batches vary in size or if t scales over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a stable online statistic with clear time/space guarantees using simple data structures.

## Key Concepts

- streaming algorithms
- two-heap median maintenance
- amortized vs worst-case costs
- space efficiency

## Code Example

```javascript
// production-ready sketch of two-heap median tracker
class BinaryHeap {
  constructor(compare){ this.data=[]; this.compare=compare; }
  size(){ return this.data.length; }
  top(){ return this.data[0]; }
  push(x){ this.data.push(x); this._siftUp(this.data.length-1); }
  pop(){ const top=this.data[0]; const end=this.data.pop(); if (this.data.length){ this.data[0]=end; this._siftDown(0); } return top; }
  _siftUp(i){ const x=this.data[i]; while(i>0){ const p=(i-1)>>1; if (this.compare(x, this.data[p])>=0) break; this.data[i]=this.data[p]; i=p; } this.data[i]=x; }
  _siftDown(i){ const n=this.data.length; const x=this.data[i]; while(true){ let l=2*i+1, r=l+1, smallest=i; if (l<n && this.compare(this.data[l], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=l; if (r<n && this.compare(this.data[r], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=r; if (smallest===i) break; this.data[i]=this.data[smallest]; i=smallest; } this.data[i]=x; }
}

class MedianTracker {
  constructor(){ this.lower=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> b-a); this.upper=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> a-b); }
  insert(x){ if (this.lower.size()===0 || x <= this.lower.top()) this.lower.push(x); else this.upper.push(x);
    if (this.lower.size() > this.upper.size()+1) this.upper.push(this.lower.pop());
    else if (this.upper.size() > this.lower.size()) this.lower.push(this.upper.pop());
  }
  median(){ if (this.lower.size() === this.upper.size()) return (this.lower.top() + this.upper.top())/2; return this.lower.top(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for deletions or outliers removal?
- How would you validate correctness with unit tests and random streams?

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming data structures with sliding windows, handling batched updates, and rigorous amortized analysis. Also probes correctness under expirations and the practical choice between exact maintenance vs rebuilds.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window frequency tables, heap with lazy deletion, batch amortized analysis, memory locality, worst-case scenarios.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopFreqWindow {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.cnt = new Map(); this.queue = []; this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  batchInsert(vals) {
    for (const v of vals) {
      this.queue.push(v);
      this.cnt.set(v, (this.cnt.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.heap.push({ value: v, count: this.cnt.get(v) });
    }
    this.expireIfNeeded();
  }
  expireIfNeeded() {
    while (this.queue.length > this.W) {
      const old = this.queue.shift();
      const c = (this.cnt.get(old) || 0) - 1;
      if (c <= 0) this.cnt.delete(old); else this.cnt.set(old, c);
      this.heap.push({ value: old, count: c });
    }
  }
  top1() {
    while (true) {
      const t = this.heap.peek(); if (!t) return null;
      if ((this.cnt.get(t.value) || 0) === t.count) return t.value;
      this.heap.pop();
    }
  }
}
```

